.TH BCRAFT-RECIPE 5 "September 2026" "bcraft 0.6.0" "Basecraft Linux"
.SH NAME
bcraft-recipe \- create source recipes and binary packages for bcraft
.SH DESCRIPTION
A bcraft source package is intentionally small.  It normally contains a
package.conf file, an executable build.sh file, and optionally patches or files
that belong directly to the package.
.PP
The package author does not write MANIFEST or PKGINFO by hand.  bcraft creates
both files automatically after build.sh has installed the package into PKGDIR.
.PP
This model is intended to make it easy to translate Linux From Scratch (LFS)
and Beyond Linux From Scratch (BLFS) build instructions into reproducible
Basecraft package recipes.
.SH RECIPE LAYOUT
A simple archive based recipe may look like:
.PP
.nf
joe/
|-- package.conf
`-- build.sh
.fi
.PP
A recipe that carries files directly may look like:
.PP
.nf
bcraft/
|-- package.conf
|-- build.sh
`-- files/
    `-- bcraft
.fi
.SH PACKAGE.CONF
package.conf is sourced by bcraft as a shell fragment.
.PP
The following fields are required:
.TP
.B name
Package name.
.TP
.B version
Upstream package version.
.TP
.B release
Basecraft packaging release.
.TP
.B arch
Target architecture such as x86_64, or any for architecture independent
packages.
.TP
.B base
Required Basecraft base identifier such as basecraft-1, or any for bootstrap
packages that are not bound to one Basecraft base.
.TP
.B description
Short package description.
.TP
.B license
Optional license identifier.  SPDX identifiers are recommended, for example
GPL-3.0-or-later, LGPL-3.0-or-later, MIT or BSD-2-Clause.  If omitted, bcraft records
license=unknown in PKGINFO.
.TP
.B maintainer
Optional maintainer string, for example:
.PP
.nf
maintainer="Marco Welter <mawe@tamaly.de>"
.fi
.TP
.B depends
Optional shell array containing package names required at install/update time,
for example:
.PP
.nf
depends=(
    "ncurses"
    "libfoo"
)
.fi
.TP
.B source_mode
archive, local, or recipe.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
The optional depends array provides deliberately simple dependency metadata.
For each entry, bcraft writes one depend= line to the generated PKGINFO:
.PP
.nf
depend=ncurses
depend=libfoo
.fi
.PP
When the binary package is installed or updated, bcraft checks whether each
dependency is registered below /var/lib/basecraft/packages.  It does not
resolve versions, alternatives, providers, or dependency chains and it does not
automatically install missing packages.
.PP
On an existing LFS/BLFS system a required component may already be installed
without being managed by bcraft.  In that case the administrator can explicitly
skip the dependency check with --nodeps:
.PP
.nf
bcraft install --nodeps foo-1.0-1.bcraft
.fi
.PP
--nodeps skips dependency checking and, for install, permits installation over
an existing bcraft registration and existing payload paths. Existing payload
paths are overwritten. Architecture, Basecraft base, package integrity, and
package structure validation remain active.
.SH SOURCES
Existing single-source archive recipes remain valid:
.PP
.nf
source_mode="archive"
source_url="https://example.org/foo-1.0.tar.xz"
source_file="foo-1.0.tar.xz"
source_sha256="..."
.fi
.PP
For multiple remote sources use parallel arrays. Entries with the same index
belong together:
.PP
.nf
source_mode="archive"
source_urls=(
    "https://example.org/foo-1.0.tar.xz"
    "https://example.org/foo-fix.patch"
)
source_files=(
    "foo-1.0.tar.xz"
    "foo-fix.patch"
)
source_sha256s=(
    "SHA256-OF-ARCHIVE"
    "SHA256-OF-PATCH"
)
.fi
.PP
bcraft downloads every source into /var/cache/basecraft/sources and verifies
each SHA-256 checksum. Only source_files[0] is automatically extracted. Every
additional source is copied unchanged into SRCDIR using its declared filename.
For example build.sh can use:
.PP
.nf
cd "$SRCDIR"
patch -Np1 -i foo-fix.patch
.fi
.PP
The three archive arrays must contain the same number of entries. Source
filenames must be plain basenames and may not contain directory components.
.SH LOCAL SOURCES
For sources shipped inside the bcraft source package use source_mode="local".
Files are stored below the recipe's sources/ directory:
.PP
.nf
source_mode="local"
source_files=(
    "foo-1.0.tar.xz"
    "foo-fix.patch"
)
source_sha256s=(
    "SHA256-OF-ARCHIVE"
    "SHA256-OF-PATCH"
)
.fi
.PP
Local sources are verified exactly like downloaded sources. The first file is
extracted as the primary source and additional files are copied unchanged into
SRCDIR. source_files and source_sha256s must have equal lengths.
.SH RECIPE SOURCES
With source_mode="recipe", source files are contained directly in the source
package. This remains useful for shell scripts, Basecraft tools, configuration
packages and other small local projects.
.SH SRC-BUILD AND AUTO CHECKSUMS
A source package can be created directly from a recipe directory with:
.PP
.nf
bcraft src-build [recipe-dir]
.fi
.PP
The directory must contain at least package.conf and build.sh. If recipe-dir is omitted, the current directory is used. The generated file is named name-version-release.src.bcraft.
.PP
For archive sources, source_sha256 may be set to AUTO while authoring the recipe:
.PP
.nf
source_sha256="AUTO"
.fi
.PP
src-build downloads source_url/source_file, calculates SHA-256 and replaces AUTO only in the temporary package.conf copy stored in the generated source package. The original recipe remains unchanged. In a multi-source recipe, individual entries in source_sha256s may likewise be AUTO and are resolved independently.
.PP
AUTO is an authoring convenience only. A normal bcraft build requires fixed 64-digit SHA-256 values and rejects AUTO. AUTO is not supported for source_mode="local" because those sources are already part of the recipe.
.SH BUILD.SH
build.sh is executed by bcraft and must install the complete package into
PKGDIR.  It must not install directly into the running system.
.PP
bcraft exports these variables:
.TP
.B SRCDIR
Extracted upstream source directory, or the recipe directory for
source_mode="recipe".
.TP
.B BUILDDIR
Optional separate build directory.
.TP
.B PKGDIR
Package staging root.  All package files must end up below this directory.
.TP
.B RECIPE_DIR
Directory containing package.conf and build.sh.
.TP
.B WORKDIR
Current package build tree.
.TP
.B JOBS
Suggested parallel build job count.
.TP
.B PKGNAME
Package name.
.TP
.B PKGVER
Package version.
.TP
.B PKGRELEASE
Package release.
.SH TRANSLATING LFS OR BLFS INSTRUCTIONS
For many packages an LFS/BLFS recipe can be copied almost literally.
The important change is that the install step must target PKGDIR.
.PP
For example, LFS/BLFS may contain:
.PP
.nf
./configure --prefix=/usr
make
make install
.fi
.PP
A bcraft build.sh becomes:
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

cd "$SRCDIR"

./configure --prefix=/usr
make -j"$JOBS"
make DESTDIR="$PKGDIR" install
.fi
.PP
If an upstream Makefile does not support DESTDIR, install individual files
explicitly:
.PP
.nf
install -Dm755 foo "$PKGDIR/usr/bin/foo"
install -Dm644 foo.conf "$PKGDIR/etc/foo/foo.conf"
.fi
.PP
Commands that modify the running system, for example systemctl, useradd,
ldconfig or direct writes to /usr or /etc, must not simply be copied into
build.sh.
.SH OUT-OF-TREE BUILDS
Projects may use BUILDDIR when supported.  For CMake, for example:
.PP
.nf
cmake -S "$SRCDIR" -B "$BUILDDIR" \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr

cmake --build "$BUILDDIR" -j "$JOBS"

DESTDIR="$PKGDIR" \
    cmake --install "$BUILDDIR"
.fi
.PP
A separate build directory is optional.  Correct staging into PKGDIR is the
important requirement.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
Regular files below etc/ are automatically classified as configuration files.
Additional configuration files outside /etc may be declared in package.conf:
.PP
.nf
config_files=(
    "opt/foo/settings.conf"
    "usr/share/foo/local.conf"
)
.fi
.PP
During updates, unchanged configuration files are replaced normally.  Locally
modified files are preserved and the new package version is installed as
FILE.bcraft-new.
.SH MANIFEST GENERATION
After build.sh completes, bcraft walks PKGDIR and creates MANIFEST
automatically.
.PP
Current manifest entries have three tab separated fields:
.PP
.nf
TYPE    SHA256-or--    PATH
.fi
.PP
Types are:
.TP
.B f
Regular package file.
.TP
.B c
Configuration file.
.TP
.B l
Symbolic link.
.TP
.B d
Directory.
.PP
Only configuration files require a stored per-file SHA-256 hash for update and
remove decisions.  Other entries use - in the checksum field.
.PP
Because the manifest is generated from the staged package tree, it records what
the package actually installs rather than what the package author expected it
to install.
.SH SOURCE PACKAGE CREATION
After preparing a recipe directory, create the source package with tar:
.PP
.nf
tar -czf foo-1.0-1.src.bcraft foo/
.fi
.PP
Build it with:
.PP
.nf
bcraft build foo-1.0-1.src.bcraft
.fi
.PP
The resulting binary package is written below:
.PP
.nf
/var/cache/basecraft/packages/
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE: JOE
package.conf:
.PP
.nf
name="joe"
version="4.8"
release="1"
arch="x86_64"
base="basecraft-1"
description="JOE - Joe's Own Editor"
license="GPL-3.0-or-later"
maintainer="Your Name <you@example.org>"

depends=(
    "ncurses"
)

source_mode="archive"
source_url="https://example.invalid/joe-4.8.tar.gz"
source_file="joe-4.8.tar.gz"
source_sha256="REPLACE_WITH_REAL_SHA256"
.fi
.PP
build.sh:
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

cd "$SRCDIR"

./configure \
    --prefix=/usr \
    --sysconfdir=/etc \
    --docdir="/usr/share/doc/${PKGNAME}-${PKGVER}"

make -j"$JOBS"
make DESTDIR="$PKGDIR" install
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I package.conf
Recipe metadata.
.TP
.I build.sh
Package build and staging instructions.
.TP
.I patches/
Optional package patches.
.TP
.I files/
Optional files carried directly by the recipe.
